// The retransmission timeout marks the head and, of the rest, only what was last sent at least RACK's round trip ago
// (RFC 8985 6.3; Linux's tcp_timeout_mark_lost); later segments are left to RACK. The handshake's ACK 10 ms after the
// SYN-ACK gives RACK its first round trip, 10 ms, and an RTO of 210 ms. Three segments leave at 10 ms; nothing answers,
// so the tail probe sends the third again two round trips later, at 30 ms (RFC 8985 7.2), and the RTO runs 210 ms from
// the probe, to 240 ms. Two more leave at 235 ms, 5 ms before it: the probe is scheduled again for min(235 + 20, 240),
// the RTO's moment, and none goes while the first is in flight, so the expiry at 240 ms is the RTO's. The head is
// resent alone, the second, 230 ms old, and the probed third, 210 ms old, are marked lost, and the fourth and fifth,
// 5 ms old, are not. The resend's ACK at 245 ms comes sooner than the minimum round trip after the resend, so it may
// be the original's and moves none of RACK's state; in the loss state the window is what was in flight after the
// marking, 200, plus one segment, 1648, which no ACK grew since nothing filled it, and the pass sends the two entries
// the timeout marked and nothing else. The ACK of everything ends the loss state. Linux's probe goes 2 ms later, at
// 32 ms, and its RTO 210 ms after it; the writes at 235 ms schedule its probe again, at 257 ms, without the RTO's cap,
// which it counts from the head's last transmission and finds already past (tcp_rto_delta_us); at 257 ms the probe
// gives way to that overdue RTO, which fires a tick later, and the fourth and fifth, more than RACK's round trip old by
// then, are marked lost too.
